Implement the following changes to the DevOps job runner and dashboard application.

1. Session history username column
Add a username field to the session record, captured at login time (not looked up live), so historical session rows keep the name of the user who was logged in even if the account is later renamed or removed. Update the session history table/API and UI to show this username column alongside the existing session ID, login timestamp, status, and logout time columns, positioned near the user ID column.

2. Stop triggering widget data fetches on page load
Currently, loading the dashboard page - including a fresh login, a new user's first login, or a browser refresh - triggers a live fetch/run of the underlying jobs behind each dashboard widget. This is incorrect: viewing the dashboard should never itself trigger a job run. Remove any fetch-on-mount behavior from the dashboard widgets. The dashboard should only ever read and display whatever data is currently cached; it must never call the job execution path directly from a page load or component mount.

3. New persistent widget cache
Create a new SQLite database file, jobs.db, dedicated to this cache. Add a table keyed by widget and application ID storing: the widget's last-fetched data (JSON), the timestamp it was last successfully updated, and how that update was triggered (scheduled or manual). This must be durable across application restarts and redeployments - it should not live only in memory.

4. Wall-clock aligned scheduler
Replace all existing per-widget refresh timers (including any 5/10-minute intervals currently in use) with a single unified schedule: every widget refreshes every 15 minutes, aligned to the actual clock in Indian Standard Time - i.e. at :00, :15, :30, and :45 past the hour - rather than counting 15 minutes from whenever the app or a session started. Use a cron-style scheduler trigger for this (for example APScheduler's CronTrigger with minute='0,15,30,45' and timezone='Asia/Kolkata') rather than a sleep/interval loop, so timing is independent of app start time. The application runs as a single instance, so no distributed locking or leader election is needed for the scheduler.

5. Startup and reconnect behavior
On application startup (including after a restart or redeployment), for each widget/application entry in jobs.db: if the cached data is less than 15 minutes old, keep using it as-is and simply resume the normal :00/:15/:30/:45 schedule going forward. If the cached data is 15 minutes old or older (for example the app was stopped for an extended period), perform one immediate catch-up fetch for that entry only, store the result, and then resume the normal schedule. Do not force a full refresh of every widget/application just because the app restarted - only the entries that are actually stale.

6. Login and page load behavior
A new user logging in, any user's browser refresh, or simply navigating to the dashboard must never trigger a fetch. These events should only read the current contents of jobs.db and render them. The only two things that should ever trigger an actual job run for a widget are the scheduled :00/:15/:30/:45 tick and the manual refresh button.

7. Manual refresh button
The manual refresh button should still fetch immediately and update jobs.db, but it must not reset or restart the wall-clock schedule - the next scheduled tick still lands on the next :00/:15/:30/:45 mark regardless of when manual refresh was last used.

8. Trigger source tagging and history cleanliness
Every widget refresh recorded in jobs.db (and any corresponding underlying job run) should be tagged with its trigger source: scheduled or manual. Scheduled runs are system-initiated and must not be attached to any individual user's session ID, so they no longer appear inside a user's personal session or activity history. Manual refreshes remain attributed to the user who triggered them, as today.

Please review the current dashboard widget refresh code end to end, identify every place a widget fetch is currently triggered by page load, mount, or login, and remove those triggers as part of this change - then confirm the dashboard's data flow now matches: read from jobs.db on view, write to jobs.db only from the scheduler tick or the manual refresh action.
